Reworks the touch layer so gestures survive, so multiple units can be
selected, and so a double tap exists at all.

Four things were wrong.

1. The camera reset itself the moment a finger left the glass. The gestures
   moved the camera by translating its matrix directly, but CCameraController
   owns the authoritative state in six CSmoothedValues and rebuilds that
   matrix from them in CGameView::Update every frame. During a gesture
   IsGestureActive() makes Update return early, so the movement was visible;
   as soon as the gesture ended, Update ran again and restored the camera from
   values that had never heard of it. Panning and zooming now read and write
   through CGameView::SetCamera, which writes the smoothed values with
   SetValue (target and current together) and rebuilds the matrix without
   smoothing - immediate, and it stays.

2. SDL_FingerID was used straight as an index into two-element arrays. It is
   an opaque, ever-increasing id, not a slot number, so after a couple of
   touches this wrote past the end of m_Down/m_Pos/m_Prev. Ids are mapped to
   slots now.

3. There was no way to select more than one unit. input.js has had rubber-band
   selection all along (INPUT_SELECTING -> INPUT_BANDBOXING), but one-finger
   dragging was spent on the camera, so it could never be reached. One finger
   now draws the selection rectangle as a synthetic left-drag, and the world
   moves under two fingers instead, which is also the way round an RTS wants:
   selecting happens far more often than scrolling. Pinching zooms about the
   midpoint. touch.onefingerpan puts the old mapping back.

4. Taps always reported clicks = 1, so the double tap input.js implements -
   every unit of this type on screen - and the triple tap were dead. SDL never
   counts clicks for fingers, so the taps are counted here.

Also: the drag threshold was 16 px, about a millimetre on a phone screen and
well inside a finger's own wobble, so nearly every tap became a drag. It is
now 40 px and configurable. A mouse motion is emitted when the finger goes
down, not just before the click, so the GUI's tooltip timer runs while a
finger rests on a build or upgrade button - lift to read the tooltip, keep
holding for the right click. And the pinch is clamped to view.zoom.min/max,
which never mattered while the zoom was being thrown away and does now.

